  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">The Tuvalu Ministry of Public Works &amp; Infrastructure, Environment, Labour, Meteorology &amp; Disaster has approved and is utilizing the tool Guidelines on Standard Employment Terms in order to inform decision making on engagement with labour mobility initiatives. The guidelines are captured in the report &quot;Towards establishing standard employment contracts for migrant workers from Tuvalu and Kiribati&quot; which:
&#x2022;	Identifies standard terms that ILO Conventions and Recommendations indicate should be included in the contracts of migrant workers in the agriculture, fisheries, hospitality and maritime sectors; and 
&#x2022;	Recommends content of the terms identified in part (a) above based on international labour standards, together with information on minimum requirements under current employment law in Australia and New Zealand.
The guidance tool will contribute to the alignment between migrant worker contracts and both national and international standards. For example, the tool is used by Labour Mobility Liaison Officers based in countries of destination to guide migrant workers on their rights and entitlements.  The guidelines clarify the minimum legal employment and labour conditions for migrant workers in Australia and New Zealand. 
Ministry of Public Works &amp; Infrastructure, Environment, Labour, Meteorology &amp; Disaster is also accessing information in the report on standard employment terms in order to guide support to individual migrant workers, contributing to the protection of migrant workers. ILO provided technical assistance and oversight to the report on standard employment, which served as a bedrock of the Guidelines on Standard Employment Terms. The report was developed through consultation workshops and desk research organized and overseen by ILO.  This report provides guidance on standard employment contracts for migrant workers from Kiribati and Tuvalu, with particular reference to Australia and New Zealand (being the main destination countries). The validation process for the Guidelines on Standards Employment Terms was also leveraged as an opportunity to train tripartite constituents on standard employment terms and minimum standards in relation to employment law in Australia and New Zealand.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="FR">7.5.1. Number of member States with labour migration frameworks or institutional mechanisms to protect the labour rights of migrant workers and promote coherence with employment, skills, social protection, and other relevant policies</narrative>
